{{also|Hunt}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|hunten}}, from {{inh|en|ang|huntian||to hunt}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*huntōn||to hunt, capture}}, possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱent-||to catch, seize}}. Related to {{cog|goh|hunda||booty}}, {{cog|got|𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃||body of captives}}, {{cog|ang|hūþ||plunder, booty, prey}}, {{cog|ang|hentan||to catch, seize}}. More at {{l|en|hent}}, {{l|en|hint}}.
In some areas read as a collective form of {{m|en|hound}} by {{l|en|folk etymology}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/hʌnt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-hunt.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌnt|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To find or search for an animal in the wild with the intention of killing the animal for its meat or for sport.
#: {{ux|en|State Wildlife Management areas often offer licensed hunters the opportunity to '''hunt''' on public lands.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her uncle will go out and '''hunt''' for deer, now that it is open season.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Genesis|27|5|passage{{w|Esau}} went to the field to '''hunt''' for venison.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=II|poem=Locksley Hall|page=100|passage=Like a dog, he '''hunts''' in dreams, and thou art staring at the wall, / Where the dying night-lamp flickers, and the shadows rise and fall.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1981|journal=Field & Stream|volume=86|issue=5|page=107
|passage=Either the bird will be downgraded to "threatened" status — which means it can be '''hunted''' — or it will be declared a nonspecies, as has already happened to all its taxonomic kissing cousins.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|title=Backyard deer hunting: converting deer to dinner for pennies per pound|page=10|isbn=1449084354}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To try to find something; search (for).
#: {{ux|en|The little girl was '''hunting''' for shells on the beach.}}
#: {{ux|en|The police are '''hunting''' for evidence.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|act=I|scene=i|passage=He after honour '''hunts''', I after love.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=I stumbled along through the young pines and huckleberry bushes. Pretty soon I struck into a sort of path that, I cal'lated, might lead to the road I was '''hunting''' for. It twisted and turned, and, the first thing I knew, made a sudden bend around a bunch of bayberry scrub and opened out into a big clear space like a lawn.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Prill Boyle|title=Defying Gravity: A Celebration of Late-Blooming Women|isbn=1578601541|page=119|passage=My idea of retirement was to hunt seashells, play golf, and do a lot of walking.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Ann Major|title=Nobody's Child|isbn=1459271939|passage=What kind of woman came to an island and stayed there through a violent storm and then got up the next morning to '''hunt''' seashells? She had fine, delicate features with high cheekbones and the greenest eyes he'd ever seen.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To drive; to chase; with ''down'', ''from'', ''away'', etc.
#: {{ux|en|to '''hunt''' down a criminal}}
#: {{ux|en|He was '''hunted''' from the parish.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To use or manage (dogs, horses, etc.) in hunting.
#: {{ux|en|Did you '''hunt''' that pony last week?}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|issue=104|date=4 July 1711|passage=He '''hunts''' a pack of dogs better than any man in the country.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To use or traverse in pursuit of game.
#: {{ux|en|He '''hunts''' the woods, or the country.}}
# {{lb|en|bell-ringing|transitive}} To move or shift the order of (a [[bell]]) in a regular course of [[change]]s.
# {{lb|en|bell-ringing|intransitive}} To shift up and down in order regularly.
# {{lb|en|engineering|intransitive}} To be in a state of instability of movement or forced [[oscillation]], as a [[governor]] which has a large movement of the balls for small change of load, an arc-lamp clutch mechanism which moves rapidly up and down with variations of current, etc.; also, to [[seesaw]], as a pair of [[alternator]]s working in parallel.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Bernard Wilkie|title=Special Effects in Television|page=174|passage={{...}} after which the inertia of the camera causes the motor to '''hunt''' with fluctuating speed.}}

